From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] The navigation bar gets its own key: one tap puts it away

The reader asked to own the bar's visibility. This patch gives the bar one more key at the
right-hand end: a chevron pointing down onto a line. A tap toggles the bar -- patch 0076
owns the state, applies it, and puts the bar back on a swipe up from the bottom edge.

The nav bar's layout is a string from SystemUI's own resources -- `config_navBarLayout` in
`packages/SystemUI/res/values/config.xml` -- and its keys are inflated from it by
`NavigationBarInflaterView`, so this is a new token (`navlock`) and a new layout for it, not
a change to framework-res: no resource ID that the ART boot image depends on moves. The
button is a plain `ImageView` rather than a `KeyButtonView`, because it sends no key code.

The layout this patch started with put the fourth key inside the same weighted row as the
other three, `recent[.5WC],navlock[1WC]`, which is where the keys began to collide on the
device: the row's middle is a spacer of weight 1 (`addGravitySpacer()`), the weights before
it (0.25 + 0.5) no longer added up to the weights after it (0.5 + 1 + 0.25), so the spacer
sat 81.7 dp left of the panel's centre while the home key -- weight 0, 70 dp, centred by
`center_group` -- stayed on the centre. The home key overlapped the recents key by 59 px,
and the touch area of the recents key under it went to Home. The layout is now

    ;space[0.25W],back[1WC],home[1WC],recent[1WC],space[0.25W];navlock[0.15W]

which the same inflater reads as: three keys of weight 1 each in the centre group, so their
centres are 289.1 px apart and `home` lands on the panel's centre 506.0 px; an invisible
`space` of weight 0.25 at either end of that group, which keeps the three keys' margins
equal (72 px each side at this density) and therefore the home key on the centre; and
`navlock` alone in the end group, weight 0.15, which is 132 px of frame -- just over the
key's 70 dp, so the key fills it. It carries no `C`, and an end-group token without `C` is
laid out at the frame's end, so the key sits on the right edge itself instead of floating
in a frame twice its width.

The string starts with `;`, and that is not a typo: `inflateLayout()` splits on `;` into
three groups and inflates the first into the ends group *before* the gravity spacer of
weight 1, so a leading `space` there is not the mirror of the trailing one -- it moves the
spacer and puts `home` 39 px left of the panel's centre. The empty start group makes both
spaces the centre group's own, one at each end of the three keys.

Measured on this panel (1072 x 1448 px, density 1.875): usable strip 1012 px, key centres
at 217 / 506 / 795 px, keys 131 px wide (70 dp), navlock's key at 881..1012 px, 20 px clear
of the recents key. No two keys overlap, which is what the first form of this patch got
wrong: `home` and `recent` were 59 px into each other, and a tap in the strip they shared
went to `home`, so that much of the recents key could not be pressed.

The key does not touch Settings itself: it sends the broadcast introduced by patch 0076 and
the policy side owns the state, so there is one place that decides.

The icon is a vector declared at 22 dp with a 24-unit viewport, and the key's vertical
padding is 13 dp, leaving the key's full 22 dp of content: the chevron is drawn at 22 dp,
41 px on this panel, rather than being scaled down to the 18 dp the other keys' 15 dp of
padding leaves them. Not run on a device yet: whether the chevron and the line read on
e-paper is the thing to look at, and a 15 dp padding puts it back with the other three.

Not run on a device yet when this patch was written; how to run it is in
`readom-port/reports/research/t34-nav-impl.md`.

